WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.320
Violence, though deeply rooted
in our evolutionary history,

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.800
has to be one of the most destructive
of human behaviours.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.480
For years, researchers
have tried to understand

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.200
what drives us to commit
acts of violence.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.800
Is it the environment
we were raised in?

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.920
The socio-economic situation
we find ourselves in?

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.240
Or is it driven by something
completely different -

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.080
something we have no control of -

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:26.640
our genes?

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.640
Or specifically, the so-called
'warrior gene'?

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.040
The story of the warrior gene
starts in 1978,

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.800
when a woman walked into a hospital
in the Netherlands,

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.120
and not because she was
worried about herself,

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.080
but because she was worried
about her family.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.120
All the men in the family

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.880
had a history of really terrible

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:53.920
violent crimes.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.120
There were murders, there were rapes,

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.560
going back generations
and generations.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.160
Researchers started gathering
DNA samples

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.200
from the members of her family,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.680
and they discovered that all the men

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.720
who had this history
of very violent crime,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.840
they had the same genetic change

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:17.040
in a gene called MAOA,
or monoamine oxidase A,

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.720
that completely knocked out
the function of the gene,

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.160
it wasn't working at all.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.360
We all have two copies
of this 'warrior gene'.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.920
It makes a molecule in your brain

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.920
that breaks down a chemical
called serotonin.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.320
This is a neurotransmitter,

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.080
it sends signals between
the cells in your brain,

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.760
and affects your behaviour.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.040
So if this gene isn't working
as well as it could do,

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.680
it's thought that perhaps it makes
you more impulsive,

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.440
it makes it harder to control urges.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.880
So the Dutch study was the first time
that this gene, MAOA,

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.440
had been linked to violent behaviour,

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.600
but then a study in New Zealand
took this further.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:01.120
The Dunedin study followed
1,000 New Zealanders for decades,

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.080
looking for connections between
childhood experiences

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.480
and violent behaviour later in life.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.280
They found that having a variation

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.400
that just lowers the level
of activity of this gene,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.440
not knocks it out completely,
but just lowers the activity,

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:19.160
was associated with
a history of violence.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.720
But curiously, mostly only in people

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.760
who also had a very
difficult childhood as well.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.720
And that caught the eye
of researchers in Finland

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.840
working with prisoners in
a very high security prison.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.720
We collected a cohort

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.520
of around 800 prisoners,

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.080
and about two-thirds of them

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.640
had conducted
at least one violent crime.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:49.440
But a little bit more than 10% or 15%

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.480
had committed at least 10
violent crimes,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.440
murders, attempted murders,
manslaughters.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.520
I was sceptical, but what we did find

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:04.160
was indeed if an individual had two
or more violent crimes

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:10.120
then he or she had significantly more
of the low activity MAOA.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.760
We know that up to six in ten people 

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.480
are walking around with a low
activity version of MAOA,

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:22.640
but of course, six out of ten people
don't commit horrific violent crimes.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.920
So obviously we can't say that
if you've got this gene variation

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.960
you are going to be
a violent criminal,

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.640
genetics doesn't
really work like that.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:37.840
But this idea that there's a gene
behind violent behaviour,

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.560
could you use that
as a defence in court?

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.240
You know, "It wasn't me, guv. It was
my genes that made me do it."

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.120
Well it might sound a bit strange,

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.720
but that is actually
what's happening.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.680
It's only really been used

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.000
in two countries,

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.440
two in an Italian court

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.560
and the rest have been
in American courts.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.360
It's been used as a way of saying
that the person lacked control.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:06.440
I think it's the sort of evidence
that is attempting to be used

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:08.400
to dazzle a jury.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.960
In the US, where you have
the death penalty

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.400
for these kinds of incredibly
violent crimes,

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:16.840
the stakes are obviously, literally,
life and death.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:20.600
So lawyers will try and introduce
any kind of evidence they can

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.600
to argue against the death
penalty for their client.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.680
To say that there is a gene,

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.240
like a time bomb that
none of us are aware of,

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.240
I think is wishful thinking.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.160
So humans carry different versions
of the MAOA gene,

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.040
and those that reduce
the activity of this gene

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.480
have been linked to increased risk
of aggressive behaviour.

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:44.600
Some studies also suggest a link

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:49.480
between an abusive childhood, the
MAOA gene not functioning adequately,

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:53.560
and an increased risk of developing
antisocial personality disorder,

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:57.000
which may result in committing
violent criminal acts.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.520
But whilst it can be tempting
to paint a simple picture

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.320
of how genetics influence
our behaviour,

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.640
in reality, it's an incredibly
complex issue.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:10.440
Everything that happens to us
interacts with our genes

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.600
and influences who we are
and how we behave.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.120
The fact that we can find
a connection

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.080
between genes and violent behaviour

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:21.080
doesn't mean that we're going
to be heading towards

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:25.040
some terrible genetic dystopia
where little Johnny is locked up

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.640
because he's got 'bad genes' that
might make him do bad things.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:31.680
That simply isn't how genetics work.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:35.200
We have agency, we have independence,

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.720
we can make our own free choices
about who we are and how we behave.

